Sorry to be the one to resurrect an almost 10-year old thread...but does anyone know if you can either (1) fix parameters (i.e. epsilon and gamma) in unmarked (I can't find documentation of this anywhere) or (2) if the 'unadorned' likelihood is indeed constructed by both occu and colext?

Thanks in advance!
Kristin

On Friday, January 23, 2015 at 2:05:19 AM UTC+1 Jim Baldwin wrote:
> Good points.  I definitely agree.  In this case I'm pretty sure (but not completely certain) that the "unadorned" likelihood is constructed in both occu and colext (meaning that the constant binomial coefficients are not included in the likelihood) and therefore comparable as the results have matched exactly when I've repeated the calculations outside of unmarked.
>
> Jim
>
>
> On Wed, Jan 21, 2015 at 11:54 PM, Kery Marc <marc...@vogelwarte.ch> wrote:
> >
> > Dear Jim,
> >
> >
> >
> > just one comment: most of the times one cannot compare deviances or AIC scores across different model fitting functions in unmarked (nor between different software programs in general), since the "likelihood structure" may be different.  Hence, unless Richard Chandler, Ian Fiske, or Andy Royle confirm that this is indeed possible for occu() and colext(), I would not.
> >
> >
> >
> > So: as a test of closure, I'd simply cheat on unmarked and do that in PRESENCE or MARK, where you can fix params within the same model fitting function.
> >
> >
> >
> > Kind regards  --- Marc
